import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>2.5c — Math Class: Damage Formulas & Distance</h1>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Expand Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        RPGs run on math formulas behind the scenes. Damage isn't just a flat number —
        it's modified by stats, randomness, distance, and defense. Today you implement
        two core RPG formulas and combine them.
    </p>

    <h3>Build 1: Damage Calculation Engine</h3>

    <p>Create <code>DamageCalc.java</code>. The formula for physical damage is:</p>
    <pre><code>rawDamage = weaponDamage + (strength * 0.5)
// Add randomness: rawDamage * random factor between 0.85 and 1.15
// Subtract defense: final = rawDamage - (defense * 0.3)
// Minimum: 1 damage always</code></pre>

    <p>The formulas are in the <a href="../docs/unit-02/math-class.html">Math docs</a>. You need to figure out which Math methods to use for each step — abs, min, max, pow, sqrt, or random.</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>The random factor needs to be between 0.85 and 1.15. Math.random() gives you 0.0 to 0.999.... How do you transform that range into 0.85 to 1.15? (Hint: multiply, then add.)</li>
            <li>After subtracting defense, damage could go negative. Which Math method prevents that?</li>
            <li>Why multiply defense by 0.3 instead of subtracting the full defense value? What would happen if defense fully negated damage every time?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <h3>Build 2: Distance Calculator</h3>

    <p>Create <code>DistanceCalc.java</code>. In grid-based RPGs, you often need to know how far apart two things are. The formula comes from geometry:</p>
    <pre><code>distance = sqrt((x1 - x2)² + (y1 - y2)²)</code></pre>

    <p>Implement this using Math methods. Test with these positions:</p>
    <pre><code>Player (0, 0), Enemy (3, 4) → distance: 5.0
Player (10, 5), Enemy (13, 9) → distance: 5.0
Player (0, 0), Enemy (1, 1) → distance: ~1.414</code></pre>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Damage Falls Off With Distance</h3>
        <p>
            Combine both programs. Modify the damage formula so damage is reduced
            when the enemy is far away:
        </p>
        <pre><code>distanceModifier = max(0.3, 1.0 - (distance / 20.0))
finalDamage = calculatedDamage * distanceModifier</code></pre>
        <p>
            Test at various distances: point-blank (distance=1), mid-range (distance=5),
            far (distance=15), extreme (distance=30). Does the modifier make sense?
            Is 0.3 a reasonable minimum, or should it be lower?
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Negative Squares?</h3>
        <p>
            When calculating distance, you square (x1 - x2). What if x1 is smaller
            than x2 — does squaring a negative number work? Yes — but why?
            What would happen if you used <code>Math.abs(x1 - x2)</code> instead of
            squaring? Would the distance formula still be correct?
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Implement a damage formula with modifiers and randomness</li>
            <li>Calculate distance between two points using sqrt and pow</li>
            <li>Combine multiple Math methods to create game mechanics</li>
            <li>Understand why squaring handles negative differences automatically</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/math-class.html">The Math Class</a> — all methods</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/arithmetic.html">Arithmetic &amp; Operators</a> — review</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
